{"id": "2026-10-17-news-trump-waives-new-russia-sanctions-law", "date": "2026-10-17", "stream": "news", "headline": "Trump waives new Russia sanctions law", "summary": "Trump signed an executive order on 16 October waiving all sanctions and tariffs of the Graham Act Russia sanctions law.", "url": "https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2026/10/implementing-the-lindsey-o-graham-sanctioning-russia-and-iran-act-of-2026/", "source_domain": "whitehouse.gov", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["official PR"], "topics": ["geopolitics", "politics"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "US President Donald Trump signed an executive order on Friday 16 October that waives the sanctions, restrictions and tariffs of the Graham Act, the Russia sanctions law he signed on 18 September. The order, posted by the White House late on Friday, waives the new duties, including tariffs of up to 100% on the five largest buyers of Russian oil and gas that would have applied to new purchases from 18 October. It also waives the law's sanctions on Russian officials, banks and the \"shadow fleet\" of tankers that carries Russian energy. The order says Trump certified to Congress that the waiver is \"in the national interests of the United States\". The White House has not said publicly what that interest is; the report to Congress explaining it has not been published. Sanctions imposed under earlier executive orders stay in force. The waiver came a week after Trump announced a deal to buy Russian diesel to lower fuel prices. Putin's envoy Kirill Dmitriev called the decision \"truly historic\", and Democratic co-author Senator Richard Blumenthal said it had \"everything to do with sucking up to Vladimir Putin\".", "why": "Congress passed the law by large bipartisan margins precisely to make pressure on Moscow harder to switch off, and less than three weeks before the 3 November midterms the president has used its own waiver clause to switch all of it off.", "thread_id": "2026-10-17-news-trump-waives-new-russia-sanctions-law", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-17", "event_date": "2026-10-16", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Donald Trump", "Graham Act", "Russia", "Ukraine"]}
{"id": "2026-10-17-news-russian-strikes-kill-eight-across-ukraine", "date": "2026-10-17", "stream": "news", "headline": "Russian strikes kill eight across Ukraine", "summary": "Russian attacks on 16 October across nine Ukrainian regions killed at least eight people and cut power to more than a million.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/17/russian-attacks-in-ukraine-kill-at-least-8-cut-power-to-1-million", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Russian drone and missile attacks on Friday 16 October killed at least eight people and wounded about 60 across nine Ukrainian regions, and cut power to more than a million people. Two people were killed in Kharkiv, two in the frontline city of Kramatorsk and two in Dnipropetrovsk region, regional officials said. In Volyn, on the border with Poland, a strike on a food-processing plant killed one person, and drones set about ten trucks on fire at a customs terminal in Kovel, on the main road from Kyiv to Poland. About 370,000 households in Volyn lost power, and the mayor of Lutsk said the blackout could last at least 24 hours. Shopping centres in Rivne and Sumy were also hit. Ukraine's energy ministry said power had been restored to more than a million people by the end of the day.", "why": "the attacks came on the same day Washington waived its new sanctions on Moscow, and they continue Russia's campaign against Ukraine's power grid as winter approaches.", "thread_id": "2026-10-17-news-russian-strikes-kill-eight-across-ukraine", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-17", "event_date": "2026-10-16", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Russia", "Ukraine", "Volyn", "Kharkiv"]}
{"id": "2026-10-17-news-abiy-installs-interim-tigray-government", "date": "2026-10-17", "stream": "news", "headline": "Abiy installs interim Tigray government", "summary": "Ethiopia's prime minister appointed a six-month interim administration for Tigray after federal forces took Mekelle.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/ethiopia-s-abiy-ahmed-appoints-interim-tigray-government/a-79683519", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Ethiopian Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed appointed an interim administration for Tigray on Friday 16 October, to run the region for six months, after federal forces pushed back a rebel alliance and took the regional capital Mekelle. Abiy's office said \"repeated efforts\" to resolve the conflict had failed and accused the Tigray People's Liberation Front (TPLF), the region's former ruling party, of starting a \"devastating war\". It listed ten tasks for the interim body, including \"free and fair elections\" within six months and the return of displaced people. The TPLF and six other armed groups launched an offensive against the federal government in September. TPLF vice-president Amanuel Assefa told AFP the region \"cannot be ruled by puppets\" of Addis Ababa.", "why": "the appointment replaces Tigray's leadership by decree, and the TPLF has already rejected it, so it is unclear whether a vote in six months can actually happen.", "thread_id": "2026-10-17-news-abiy-installs-interim-tigray-government", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-17", "event_date": "2026-10-16", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Ethiopia", "Abiy Ahmed", "Tigray", "TPLF"]}
{"id": "2026-10-17-news-amazon-clearing-falls-to-record-low", "date": "2026-10-17", "stream": "news", "headline": "Amazon clearing falls to record low", "summary": "INPE reported 3,335 km2 of Amazon deforestation from August 2025 to July 2026, the lowest since 1988.", "url": "https://www.gov.br/inpe/pt-br/assuntos/ultimas-noticias/brasil-alcanca-menores-taxas-de-desmatamento-da-serie-historica-na-amazonia-e-no-cerrado", "source_domain": "gov.br", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["new source"], "topics": ["world", "politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Brazil's space research institute INPE reported on Friday 16 October that 3,335 km² of Amazon rainforest were cleared between August 2025 and July 2026, the lowest figure since its records began in 1988. That is 41.8% less than the 5,731 km² of the previous year. The previous low was 4,571 km² in 2012. Clearing of the Cerrado, Brazil's tropical savanna, fell 23.8% to 5,517 km², also a record low for that series, which starts in 2001. INPE gives the Cerrado figure as consolidated but not the Amazon figure, which it usually revises later. The data come from PRODES, INPE's annual satellite survey, and INPE notes that it does not count fires: October's fires fall outside the period, and burned forest is not always counted as cleared.", "why": "the figure lets President Lula's government claim a major result eight days before the 25 October presidential runoff, in which DW reports Lula faces senator Flávio Bolsonaro and the two are level in the polls.", "thread_id": "2026-10-17-news-amazon-clearing-falls-to-record-low", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-17", "event_date": "2026-10-16", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Brazil", "INPE", "Amazon", "Lula"]}
{"id": "2026-10-17-news-berlin-and-paris-agree-car-industry-plan", "date": "2026-10-17", "stream": "news", "headline": "Berlin and Paris agree car-industry plan", "summary": "Germany and France agreed a joint plan to support Europe's car industry, reportedly including a softer 2035 emissions target.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/germany-france-agree-on-deal-to-support-europe-s-struggling-car-industry/a-79678595", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["economy"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Germany and France agreed on Friday 16 October on a joint plan to support Europe's car industry, which is struggling to compete with Chinese electric-car makers. In a joint statement, the two governments said the plan combines incentives for investment, research and production of electric cars in Europe with their climate goals. Handelsblatt reports that Chancellor Friedrich Merz and President Emmanuel Macron also agreed in principle that new cars should cut exhaust emissions by 90% from 2035, without having to offset the last 10%. That would weaken the EU's current 100% target for 2035. The plan needs the support of other EU states and the European Parliament to become law.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-10-17-news-berlin-and-paris-agree-car-industry-plan", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-17", "event_date": "2026-10-16", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Germany", "France", "EU"]}
{"id": "2026-10-17-news-albanian-student-protests-turn-violent", "date": "2026-10-17", "stream": "news", "headline": "Albanian student protests turn violent", "summary": "Three days of student-led clashes in Tirana against PM Edi Rama led to 152 arrests.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/albania-students-demand-political-change-and-resignation-of-prime-minister-edi-rama/a-79682217", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Student protests against Albanian Prime Minister Edi Rama turned into three days of clashes in Tirana up to Friday 16 October; police say 152 people have been arrested and 71 officers injured. Students are boycotting classes and demand that Rama, in power since 2013, resign. They complain that housing is unaffordable on a typical salary. The protests are part of a movement that began in late May against a tourism project linked to Jared Kushner, Donald Trump's son-in-law, in the protected coastal area of Zvernec. Rama says \"foreign hands\" are behind the protests.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-10-17-news-albanian-student-protests-turn-violent", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-17", "event_date": null, "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Albania", "Edi Rama", "Tirana"]}
{"id": "2026-10-17-news-france-braces-for-mass-cost-of-living-protests", "date": "2026-10-17", "stream": "news", "headline": "France braces for mass cost-of-living protests", "summary": "More than 600 demonstrations against high prices and inequality were planned across France on 17 October.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/france-braces-for-nationwide-protests/a-79685584", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "More than 600 demonstrations against high prices and inequality are planned across France on Saturday 17 October, and riot police clashed with protesters in Saint-Denis, north of Paris, in the morning. Students, trade unionists and Yellow Vest activists are taking part. A police source told AFP that authorities expect nearly 200,000 people, 40,000 of them in Paris, where police have banned rallies near the Élysée palace, the prime minister's office and the Champs-Élysées. Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu spoke of \"an atmosphere of perma-crisis: wars, inflation, and issues surrounding purchasing power\".", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-10-17-news-france-braces-for-mass-cost-of-living-protests", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-17", "event_date": "2026-10-17", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["France", "Sébastien Lecornu"]}
{"id": "2026-10-17-news-poll-puts-svp-at-record-high", "date": "2026-10-17", "stream": "news", "headline": "Poll puts SVP at record high", "summary": "The SRG Wahlbarometer by Sotomo puts the SVP at 29.9% a year before the 2027 federal elections, with FDP and Mitte losing ground.", "url": "https://sotomo.ch/de/projekte/wahlbarometer-oktober-2026/", "source_domain": "sotomo.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["new source"], "topics": ["switzerland", "politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The SVP would win 29.9% of the vote, its best result ever, if the federal elections were held now, according to the SRG Wahlbarometer poll by the research institute Sotomo, published on Friday 16 October, a year before the October 2027 elections. That is 2 points more than in 2023 and above the party's 2015 record (29.4%). The SP rises 1 point to 19.3%, its highest in almost twenty years, and the Greens 0.5 points to 10.3%. The FDP would fall 1 point to 13.3%, its worst result in a National Council election, and Die Mitte 1.5 points to 12.6%. The GLP is at 7.1% (−0.5). Only 27% of respondents now say the Federal Council's political position is \"about right\", the lowest since 2019. The poll is opt-in and online: 25,092 voters were surveyed between 10 and 23 September, the answers were weighted, and the margin of error is ±1.2 points, so the smaller shifts are within the noise.", "why": "party strength drives claims to Federal Council seats, and political scientist Michael Hermann notes that the FDP is still ahead of Die Mitte in the race for third place. Both are still far stronger in the Council of States. — Sotomo, Oct 2026", "thread_id": "2026-10-17-news-poll-puts-svp-at-record-high", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-17", "event_date": "2026-10-16", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["SVP", "SP", "FDP", "Die Mitte", "Sotomo"]}
{"id": "2026-10-17-news-children-s-rights-network-flags-child-poverty", "date": "2026-10-17", "stream": "news", "headline": "Children's-rights network flags child poverty", "summary": "Netzwerk Kinderrechte Schweiz says 114,000 children are poor and 315,000 more at risk and calls for a national strategy.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/netzwerk-kinderrechte-schweiz-fast-jedes-fuenfte-kind-ist-in-der-schweiz-armutsgefaehrdet", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["switzerland"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "About 114,000 children in Switzerland live in poverty and another 315,000 are at risk of it, the Netzwerk Kinderrechte Schweiz said in a statement reported by the SDA news agency on Saturday 17 October, and it called for a national strategy against child poverty. The children's-rights network says single-parent and large families are most exposed, and that the help a family gets depends on its canton and residence status. It asks for binding targets, a separate child dimension in the national poverty monitoring, and minimum national standards for family benefits. It also criticises the Federal Council for keeping what it calls \"a weakened solution\" for an ombuds office for children's rights, which parliament ordered in 2020.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-10-17-news-children-s-rights-network-flags-child-poverty", "first_seen_date": "2026-10-17", "event_date": "2026-10-17", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Netzwerk Kinderrechte Schweiz", "Switzerland"]}
